This is a non-renewable post for a limited duration of 4 years and open for the specific needs of a National Project. Accordingly, the external candidate selected for this post will continue to be considered an external candidate for the purposes of any future applications to the Agency. 
 Location 
ESEC, Redu, Belgium 
 Description 
 Hardware Security Evaluation Engineer in the System Security Section (TEC-SES), End-to-End Systems Division, Systems Department, Directorate of Technology, Engineering and Quality.
 The System Security Section is responsible for end-to-end system security engineering for the Agency’s missions, projects and activities across space, ground and user segments and communication links, at system, subsystem, element and equipment levels. Its responsibilities span the mission lifecycle from the study phase and definition of requirements to design, development, security integration and verification, and security service preparation, across the full stack, from the physical to the application layer. 
 To fulfil these functions, the System Security Section defines and conducts the associated research and development (R&D) activities and studies and implements the necessary security engineering standards. 
 Using its security laboratory facilities, the System Security Section performs vulnerability assessments across all areas within its remit and proposes detection methods and mitigation and protection measures using security assessment tools and equipment.
 This position will support a third-party activity aimed at developing a national hardware security evaluation capability.
 Duties 
 Your tasks and responsibilities will include:
 supporting the Project Manager by providing recommendations, guidance and plans for test and evaluation laboratory capability development and technologies for hardware security evaluation;
 regular reporting to the Project Manager on the status of the duties assigned under the project;
 engaging directly with and advising national customers and ESA experts on options for developing and maturing hardware security evaluation capabilities;
 deriving technical requirements and reviewing, assessing and evaluating project proposals, including the security design, development, implementation, qualification and validation;
 fostering new security application areas for multidisciplinary activities, placing emphasis on innovative concepts, cutting-edge technologies and system architectures in the field of hardware security evaluation;
 supporting laboratory activities as required;
 continuously following technological and scientific developments relevant to the position, particularly emerging threats, exploits and industry trends;
 contributing, as requested, to other technical activities beyond the main project within your area of expertise. 
 Technical competencies 
 Hardware attacks and fault injection
 Side-channel analysis and physical security testing
 Embedded systems and hardware security architecture
 Cryptographic hardware security
 Hardware security evaluation and certification standards
 Hardware evaluation laboratory technologies and technical analysis
 Behavioural competencies 
 Result Orientation
Operational Efficiency
Fostering Cooperation
Relationship Management
Continuous Improvement
Forward Thinking
 For more information, please refer to the ESA Core Behavioural Competencies guidebook 
 Education and professional experience 
 A master’s degree in a relevant field and, in principle, three years of relevant professional experience are required for this post. Alternatively, candidates with a bachelor’s degree and an additional four years of relevant professional experience may be considered.
 Additional requirements 
 Hands-on experience in hardware hacking, exploitation and security assessment across IT and embedded-system technologies, including but not limited to:
 side channel attacks;
 voltage fault injection;
 clock fault injection;
 electromagnetic fault injection;
 optical fault injection;
 timing attacks;
 simple, differential and correlation power analysis;
 debug port attacks.
 In-depth knowledge of:
 embedded system characterisation, hardware component imaging and probing techniques;
 fault characterisation and fault model development;
 boot loaders and their weaknesses;
 trusted platform modules, trusted execution environments and other similar technologies, including attacks and mitigations.
 Understanding of design principles for hardware analysis purposes, including:
 symmetric cryptographic algorithms;
 asymmetric pre-quantum cryptographic algorithms;
 asymmetric post-quantum cryptographic algorithms.
 In-depth knowledge of:
 applying relevant security and certification standards, including FIPS 140-3, Common Criteria, ENISA, ISO and ETSI, for testing and certification purposes;
 embedded systems and their interfaces and protocols;
 software programming.
Experience of firmware extraction and reversing techniques is desirable.
 …

This is a non-renewable post for a limited duration of 4 years and open for the specific needs of a National Project. Accordingly, the external candidate selected for this post will continue to be considered an external candidate for the purposes of any future applications to the Agency. 
 Location 
ESEC, Redu, Belgium 
 Description 
 Cryptography Evaluator Engineer in the System Security Section (TEC-SES), End-to-End Systems Division, Systems Department, Directorate of Technology, Engineering and Quality.
 The System Security Section is responsible for the end-to-end system security engineering of the Agency’s missions, projects and activities in space, ground and user segments and communication links, as well as at system, subsystem, element and equipment level. It covers missions from study phase to definition of requirements, design, development, security integration / verification, and security service preparation, across the full stack, from physical to application layer. 
 To serve these functions, the System Security Section defines and executes the associated technology R&D and studies, as well as the required security engineering standards. 
 Using security laboratory facilities, the System Security Section performs vulnerability assessments at all levels of its responsibility, proposing detection methods, mitigation and protection measures, using security assessment tools and equipment.
 This position will support a third party activity for the development of a national capability on the evaluation and certification of cryptographic algorithms and protocols.
 Duties 
 Your tasks and responsibilities will include:
 supporting the project manager by providing recommendations and guidance on the evaluation and certification of cryptographic algorithms;
 regular reporting to the Project Manager on the status of the duties assigned under the Project;
 direct engagement with and advice to national customers and ESA experts on the strengths, weaknesses and design of cryptographic algorithms and parameter selection for them;
 deriving recommendations on cryptographic algorithms in relation to use case and security strength;
 fostering new security application areas for multidisciplinary activities, placing emphasis on innovative concepts, cutting-edge technologies and system architectures in the field of crypto algorithm evaluation and certification;
 supporting the training of colleagues, as required;
 continuously following technological trends and evolutions in the scientific fields relevant for this position, and in particular on the latest emerging threats, exploits, and industry trends;
 being engaged in other technical activities outside the main project in the area of your expertise, as requested. 
 Technical competencies 
 Cryptographic Design and Protocol Engineering
 Mathematical Foundations of Cryptography
 Cryptanalysis and Cryptographic Security Assessment
 Formal Security Analysis and Proof Evaluation
 Cryptographic Parameter Selection and Implementation Security
 Cryptographic Evaluation, Certification and Standards
 Behavioural competencies 
 Result Orientation
Operational Efficiency
Fostering Cooperation
Relationship Management
Continuous Improvement
Forward Thinking
 For more information, please refer to the ESA Core Behavioural Competencies guidebook 
 Education and professional experience 
 A PhD in a relevant domain is required for this post together with 3 years of relevant professional experience.
 Additional requirements 
 Experience in:
 the design of cryptographic algorithms and protocols;
 analysis of real-world security strength of public and private key crypto algorithms;
 the justified selection of parameter sets for real world crypto algorithms;
 the certification of crypto algorithms;
 dealing with crypto evaluation facilities and good knowledge of formal crypto evaluation test standards and analysis of formal evaluation results.
 Very good knowledge of:
 mathematical foundations of modern public and private key crypto algorithms;
 number theory, abstract algebra, probability, discrete mathematics and computational complexity;
 security definitions, assumptions, and formal security proofs and how to assess them;
 how to to identify weaknesses in algorithm design and cryptographic constructions;
 differential and linear cryptanalysis, algebraic attacks, statistical attacks, and relevant attacks on public-key systems;
 how to evaluate brute-force resistance, key sizes, security margins, and attack complexity;
 how to assess randomness generation, key management, nonce handling, padding, and constant-time implementation requirements;
 how implementation flaws can undermine a mathematically sound algorithm;
 how to proof assumptions, reductions, and limitations.
 Familiarity with formal verification or symbolic protocol analysis, where relevant.
